          "text": "The pattern is best indexed by a low-symmetry, four-formula-unit cell rather than the commonly reported average tetragonal perovskite cell. A provisional triclinic ordered model gives:\n\n- \\(a=6.174\\) \u00c5, \\(b=6.561\\) \u00c5, \\(c=7.507\\) \u00c5  \n- \\(\\alpha=78.84^\\circ\\), \\(\\beta=100.44^\\circ\\), \\(\\gamma=89.69^\\circ\\)\n- \\(V\\approx293.1\\) \u00c5\u00b3, \\(Z=4\\)\n- space group \\(P1\\)\n\nRepresentative indexing includes 12.22\u00b0 (001), 13.76\u00b0 (010), 14.59\u00b0 (100), 19.68\u00b0 (110), 24.59\u00b0 (002), 25.73\u00b0 (012), 29.42\u00b0 (200), 31.69\u00b0 (112), 32.61\u00b0 (02\u22121), and 39.98\u00b0 (220).\n\n### Primary CIF\n\n```cif\ndata_SrTaO2N_ordered_candidate\n_audit_creation_method           'Powder-indexed direct-space model'\n_chemical_name_common            'strontium tantalum oxynitride'\n_chemical_formula_sum            'Sr4 Ta4 N4 O8'\n_chemical_formula_structural     'SrTaO2N'\n_cell_formula_units_Z            4\n\n_cell_length_a                    6.174\n_cell_length_b                    6.561\n_cell_length_c                    7.507\n_cell_angle_alpha                 78.84\n_cell_angle_beta                  100.44\n_cell_angle_gamma                 89.69\n_cell_volume                      293.1\n\n_symmetry_space_group_name_H-M    'P 1'\n_symmetry_Int_Tables_number       1\n\nloop_\n_symmetry_equiv_pos_as_xyz\n  'x,y,z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\n_atom_site_B_iso_or_equiv\nTa1  Ta  0.00000  0.00000  0.00000  1.0  0.40\nTa2  Ta  0.50000  0.50000  0.00000  1.0  0.40\nTa3  Ta  0.50000  0.00000  0.50000  1.0  0.40\nTa4  Ta  0.00000  0.50000  0.50000  1.0  0.40\n\nSr1  Sr  0.50000  0.00000  0.00000  1.0  0.65\nSr2  Sr  0.00000  0.50000  0.00000  1.0  0.65\nSr3  Sr  0.00000  0.00000  0.50000  1.0  0.65\nSr4  Sr  0.50000  0.50000  0.50000  1.0  0.65\n\nN1   N   0.25000  0.25000  0.00000  1.0  0.80\nN2   N   0.50000  0.25000  0.25000  1.0  0.80\nN3   N   0.25000  0.25000  0.50000  1.0  0.80\nN4   N   0.00000  0.25000  0.75000  1.0  0.80\n\nO1   O   0.75000  0.75000  0.00000  1.0  0.80\nO2   O   0.25000  0.00000  0.25000  1.0  0.80\nO3   O   0.75000  0.00000  0.75000  1.0  0.80\nO4   O   0.00000  0.75000  0.25000  1.0  0.80\nO5   O   0.50000  0.75000  0.75000  1.0  0.80\nO6   O   0.75000  0.50000  0.25000  1.0  0.80\nO7   O   0.25000  0.50000  0.75000  1.0  0.80\nO8   O   0.75000  0.75000  0.50000  1.0  0.80\n```\n\nThe lattice is well constrained by the peak positions, but the specific O/N ordering is not unique: Cu K\u03b1 diffraction has very little contrast between O and N. Permutations of the four N atoms over the twelve anion positions therefore remain surviving alternatives, including a fully occupationally disordered model with each anion position occupied by O \\(2/3\\) and N \\(1/3\\). Neutron diffraction or chemically constrained total-scattering data would be required to select the O/N arrangement unambiguously.",
